            ]
          },
          "worked": "Split the absolute-value branch at $x=-9$, retaining its restriction $x<0$:\n$$f(x)=\\begin{cases}-x-9,&x<-9\\\\x+9,&-9\\le x<0\\\\9-x^2,&x\\ge0.\\end{cases}$$\nThe two linear pieces meet at $(-9,0)$. The second approaches $(0,9)$.\n\nFor $x\\ge0$, retain the right half of the concave-down parabola $y=9-x^2$. Its vertex is $(0,9)$, and its $x$-intercept satisfies\n$$\\begin{align*}9-x^2&=0\\\\x^2&=9\\\\x&=\\pm3.\\end{align*}$$\nOnly $x=3$ satisfies $x\\ge0$. The quadratic includes $(0,9)$, so the combined graph has no hole there. Continue the left linear ray upwards to the left and the quadratic branch downwards to the right.",
